WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.664
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.664 --> 00:00:08.450
[APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; So now

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:09.447
&gt;&gt; All right

00:00:09.447 --> 00:00:10.600
&gt;&gt; Welcome back to Stanford.

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:14.637
&gt;&gt; It's great to be here and it's nice
to be back physically with people in

00:00:14.637 --> 00:00:17.096
the room so it's terrific
&gt;&gt; Yeah and

00:00:17.096 --> 00:00:21.449
as you can tell my classmates and I are so
excited that you're here with us today.

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:27.435
And this conversation is extra special for
me because your story hit so

00:00:27.435 --> 00:00:28.526
close to home.

00:00:28.526 --> 00:00:32.419
Like you, my dad is from South India,
he is an alumnus of IIT and

00:00:32.419 --> 00:00:36.754
immigrated here about 30 years ago,
with grit and determination,

00:00:36.754 --> 00:00:38.901
he's right here in the audience.

00:00:38.901 --> 00:00:48.901
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:00:56.068 --> 00:01:00.181
&gt;&gt; He's a role model for me and so are
you, so truly thank you for being here.

00:01:00.181 --> 00:01:03.660
&gt;&gt; The real pleasure is mine,
it's very nostalgic.

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:07.586
I was just telling John,
I saw a volleyball court outside Stanford

00:01:07.586 --> 00:01:11.598
was the end of my fledgling volleyball
career coming here and seeing

00:01:11.598 --> 00:01:12.576
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:01:12.576 --> 00:01:13.274
&gt;&gt; How good people were, but

00:01:13.274 --> 00:01:14.074
it's really nice to be back.

00:01:14.074 --> 00:01:15.940
&gt;&gt; Lots of talent around here.

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:21.770
And when you first became the CEO
of Google, it created quite a buzz.

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:26.252
Your Wikipedia page had over 350 edits
just in the week that you became

00:01:26.252 --> 00:01:29.225
the leader,
have you seen some of these edits?

00:01:29.225 --> 00:01:33.415
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Advice I would have for

00:01:33.415 --> 00:01:35.266
all of you is don't read
about yourself online.

00:01:35.266 --> 00:01:39.211
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Well, we thought it'd be a good way

00:01:39.211 --> 00:01:41.449
to start the conversation
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:01:41.449 --> 00:01:44.407
&gt;&gt; By visiting some of these Wikipedia

00:01:44.407 --> 00:01:49.335
edits, some of our favorite
ones with fact versus fake news

00:01:49.335 --> 00:01:51.913
your Wikipedia page edition.

00:01:51.913 --> 00:01:54.824
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; First of all someone claimed.

00:01:54.824 --> 00:01:59.650
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That you decided to join IIT at

00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:02.750
a young age at 8 years old, fact or fake?

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:07.655
&gt;&gt; I think that was fake, my parents
were tired of me they sent me to school,

00:02:07.655 --> 00:02:11.515
I think when I was about two and a half
to kindergarten I was pretty young when I

00:02:11.515 --> 00:02:13.469
came to Stanford but that is not true.

00:02:13.469 --> 00:02:17.716
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; At the same time everyone wanted to

00:02:17.716 --> 00:02:21.370
claim you from their
hometown in high school.

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:26.387
So there are quite a few
edits to your page

00:02:26.387 --> 00:02:30.821
on which high school you came from.

00:02:30.821 --> 00:02:32.717
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Where did you go?

00:02:32.717 --> 00:02:36.793
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; There are two schools which are right,

00:02:36.793 --> 00:02:40.465
but the final school is VanaVani
which is inside the campus where

00:02:40.465 --> 00:02:44.460
your dad went to college, so
that's where I went to school.

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:49.723
&gt;&gt; Wow, okay, so the person that said you
were homeschooled was definitely wrong.

00:02:49.723 --> 00:02:53.547
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I don't think I was homeschooled,

00:02:53.547 --> 00:02:55.762
unless it involved playing cricket.

00:02:55.762 --> 00:02:57.909
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I wasn't homeschooled.

00:02:57.909 --> 00:03:01.079
&gt;&gt; Well, on that note, [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:03:01.079 --> 00:03:02.062
&gt;&gt; Were you the captain of

00:03:02.062 --> 00:03:03.470
your high school cricket team?

00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:07.863
&gt;&gt; I would have loved to be
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:03:07.863 --> 00:03:09.006
&gt;&gt; But I was quite far from it, yeah.

00:03:09.006 --> 00:03:11.982
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; In another life [LAUGH].

00:03:11.982 --> 00:03:14.940
So now that we have
&gt;&gt; In the Metaverses, yeah.

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:16.683
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;.

00:03:16.683 --> 00:03:19.280
You're speaking my language [LAUGH].

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:23.781
&gt;&gt; So now that we have the fiction out of
the way, we can turn to some of the facts.

00:03:23.781 --> 00:03:26.823
You grew up in Indian with
limited access to technology and

00:03:26.823 --> 00:03:30.310
when you discovered technologies
it had a profound impact on you.

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:34.798
How did you go from an initial delay in
technology to devoting your career to

00:03:34.798 --> 00:03:36.660
access to technology for all?

00:03:38.490 --> 00:03:43.495
&gt;&gt; Growing up for
me every technology transition

00:03:43.495 --> 00:03:50.560
was very vivid even as a kid because
I had to wait a long time for it.

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:51.865
We were on a waitlist for

00:03:51.865 --> 00:03:56.700
rotary telephone it took five years to
be on the waitlist and get the form.

00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:00.323
I would go to get my grandparents
blood test results and

00:04:00.323 --> 00:04:02.935
it would be an hour round trip each way.

00:04:02.935 --> 00:04:05.722
And he would go all the way to
the hospital and they would say,

00:04:05.722 --> 00:04:07.700
it's not ready today come back tomorrow.

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:11.571
And then this phone came and I could
call and they would tell me whether

00:04:11.571 --> 00:04:15.798
the results were ready or not and so
to me, that was like, super profound.

00:04:15.798 --> 00:04:18.834
And people who would come
to our house to make calls,

00:04:18.834 --> 00:04:21.780
I saw how it created a sense of community.

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:26.863
So I've always had this vivid sense
of how technology can make a profound

00:04:26.863 --> 00:04:32.930
difference and so a lot of what I've tried
to do is bring that access to technology.

00:04:32.930 --> 00:04:36.900
What I got, I got a lot more
of it when I came to Stanford.

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:38.491
Walking to Sweet Hall at the time and

00:04:38.491 --> 00:04:41.630
seeing rows of computers
was life changing for me.

00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:47.650
And I was very inspired by Negroponte
is one laptop per child project.

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:52.588
And even today, a lot of what I'm able to
do at Google, be it make cheaper phones

00:04:52.588 --> 00:04:57.237
through Android, bring the next billion
people online or Chromebooks and

00:04:57.237 --> 00:05:02.750
try to make affordable laptops, all hit
close to that mission so, definitely.

00:05:02.750 --> 00:05:06.651
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and I can tell,
the way that you speak about technology,

00:05:06.651 --> 00:05:11.190
it had this impact on you and
it manifested in your studies as well.

00:05:11.190 --> 00:05:13.703
You got your engineering degree from IIT,
and

00:05:13.703 --> 00:05:16.910
then a master's here at
Stanford in Engineering.

00:05:16.910 --> 00:05:21.450
And we're about to pursue a career
in academia and get your PhD.

00:05:21.450 --> 00:05:23.950
Clearly that's not the path
you ended up taking.

00:05:23.950 --> 00:05:27.441
What changed your mind, why did you
decide to leave academia at the time?

00:05:27.441 --> 00:05:30.300
&gt;&gt; I still want to know I'll
have a conversation with my dad.

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:33.074
I think I disappointed him a little bit
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:05:33.074 --> 00:05:34.769
&gt;&gt; Not getting that PhD, but

00:05:34.769 --> 00:05:39.085
I literally came to Silicon Valley for
me it was asked literal, for

00:05:39.085 --> 00:05:43.553
me this was the place where
semiconductors are built, I came here for

00:05:43.553 --> 00:05:45.656
the silicon in Silicon Valley.

00:05:45.656 --> 00:05:50.472
And, I did my PhD student in material
science, I was studying semiconductor

00:05:50.472 --> 00:05:54.111
physics and definitely that's
what I thought I would do.

00:05:54.111 --> 00:05:58.479
A few things one is I was surrounded by
other grad students who had worked on at

00:05:58.479 --> 00:06:01.619
the time what was the rage,
it was a superconductors,

00:06:01.619 --> 00:06:04.090
what was called high TC superconductors.

00:06:04.090 --> 00:06:08.352
And I realized many of them had spent
their life and it didn't happen and so

00:06:08.352 --> 00:06:09.540
that gave me pause.

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:14.443
And I think something you all probably
deal with being in the Valley, there's so

00:06:14.443 --> 00:06:16.480
much happening outside.

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:20.740
There's a lot that beckons outside and
so I was definitely interested in that.

00:06:20.740 --> 00:06:25.725
I had financial reasons to go get a job
and so the combination of all that made me

00:06:25.725 --> 00:06:29.560
go out and be in industry and
in a great learning moment.

00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:33.190
The semiconductor industry
is extraordinarily cyclical.

00:06:33.190 --> 00:06:37.605
In my first year of the company at work,
we hired 3000 people and

00:06:37.605 --> 00:06:40.470
the next year we laid off 1700 people.

00:06:40.470 --> 00:06:44.190
And so you get out and
you go through that life learning.

00:06:44.190 --> 00:06:47.807
But that was the early days
of the internet too, and

00:06:47.807 --> 00:06:52.605
definitely seeing what the promise
the internet had and connecting

00:06:52.605 --> 00:06:57.508
it back to what I wanted to do was what
led me find my way back to Google.

00:06:57.508 --> 00:07:00.552
And I went to business school
along the way, not here, but

00:07:00.552 --> 00:07:04.650
another great school and
eventually made it back to the Valley.

00:07:04.650 --> 00:07:09.180
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it seems like you saw the rapid
change of technology outside of academia.

00:07:10.406 --> 00:07:14.558
&gt;&gt; Yeah, Boat Stanford itself
is a special place I think there

00:07:14.558 --> 00:07:19.209
are in most faculty here it's
a very symbiotic relationship which

00:07:19.209 --> 00:07:23.793
I've always thought is unique
amongst many places in the world.

00:07:23.793 --> 00:07:28.098
And so definitely both being in
the campus you get a good sense for

00:07:28.098 --> 00:07:30.100
what's happening outside.

00:07:30.100 --> 00:07:36.920
And going to industry here you
really get a sense for the dynamism.

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:41.284
We take it for granted but as I travel
around the world, most people outside

00:07:41.284 --> 00:07:45.180
are trying to understand how
the Valley works and how to do better.

00:07:45.180 --> 00:07:47.620
So it's an extraordinary place.

00:07:47.620 --> 00:07:52.660
&gt;&gt; Yeah and many of us here are in
a transitory period of our lives.

00:07:52.660 --> 00:07:56.250
Figuring out which industry
we want to go and impact.

00:07:56.250 --> 00:07:59.790
You've been at Google for
now quite a while, 20 years.

00:07:59.790 --> 00:08:03.342
And faced several crossroads
during your time.

00:08:03.342 --> 00:08:07.594
Sometimes choosing to leave an industry
is as difficult as choosing to stay.

00:08:07.594 --> 00:08:11.630
What drew you to stay at
Google over the past 20 years

00:08:11.630 --> 00:08:14.500
&gt;&gt; It's been very busy partly that [LAUGH]

00:08:14.500 --> 00:08:18.454
and you know I mean,
it's an extraordinary place.

00:08:18.454 --> 00:08:25.722
I think the breadth of talent you see,
the kinds of projects you get exposed to,

00:08:25.722 --> 00:08:29.749
you're on the cutting edge of everything.

00:08:29.749 --> 00:08:32.311
It's like, I'm not a surfer,
but I use this analogy.

00:08:32.311 --> 00:08:36.341
I don't know why, but it's like I've
tried a couple of times and it failed.

00:08:36.341 --> 00:08:41.771
But it feels like, surfing and being
on the edge of something all the time.

00:08:41.771 --> 00:08:43.251
So definitely I enjoy that.

00:08:43.251 --> 00:08:47.485
And a big part of it is,
if you look at our products,

00:08:47.485 --> 00:08:51.143
search works everywhere around the world.

00:08:51.143 --> 00:08:55.738
We take pride in providing it and
it's accessible to everyone as long as

00:08:55.738 --> 00:09:00.489
they have computing and connectivity
whether you're here at Stanford or

00:09:00.489 --> 00:09:03.571
kid in rural Indonesia,
Google works for you.

00:09:03.571 --> 00:09:07.465
And that's the philosophy we
bring across our products.

00:09:07.465 --> 00:09:09.541
The chi meI will be perhaps with YouTube.

00:09:09.541 --> 00:09:13.037
So we think about scale,
being able to reach people and

00:09:13.037 --> 00:09:17.691
make technology more accessible and
it's what I wanted to do in my life.

00:09:17.691 --> 00:09:19.931
And so it's a privilege to do it.

00:09:19.931 --> 00:09:20.641
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:09:20.641 --> 00:09:23.271
And there's no shortage
of products to work on.

00:09:23.271 --> 00:09:26.177
&gt;&gt; There's no shortage,
sometimes too many at Google.

00:09:26.177 --> 00:09:27.564
But that's kind of the story.

00:09:27.564 --> 00:09:28.199
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:09:28.199 --> 00:09:31.537
One of the early products that
you worked on was Chrome.

00:09:31.537 --> 00:09:35.121
You led the team and
initially there was some pushback.

00:09:35.121 --> 00:09:38.441
High development costs
competitors in the space.

00:09:38.441 --> 00:09:41.981
But you had conviction that
the browser was the way to go.

00:09:41.981 --> 00:09:43.511
Where did the conviction come from?

00:09:43.511 --> 00:09:48.071
And how did you convince skeptics
that Chrome was the future?

00:09:48.071 --> 00:09:50.466
&gt;&gt; Now that time Eric was our CEO,
Eric Schmidt.

00:09:50.466 --> 00:09:54.755
And I remember him being angry once he
said it because he realized we were trying

00:09:54.755 --> 00:09:59.061
to build a browser and he was like, do you
know what it takes to build a browser?

00:09:59.061 --> 00:10:02.561
It takes because he had gone
through the browser battles.

00:10:02.561 --> 00:10:03.670
He definitely didn't, [LAUGH].

00:10:03.670 --> 00:10:06.691
You know, was hesitant for us to do it.

00:10:06.691 --> 00:10:07.661
Partly how did I do it?

00:10:07.661 --> 00:10:09.369
I didn't tell tell people [LAUGH].

00:10:09.369 --> 00:10:13.403
For a while I just had a small team and
worked on it.

00:10:13.403 --> 00:10:20.600
And only when we had something to show,
we had a chance to show the product and

00:10:20.600 --> 00:10:25.791
that got people excited and
so but it's a good lesson.

00:10:25.791 --> 00:10:30.442
I think if you have a set of committed
people, passionate people you can achieve

00:10:30.442 --> 00:10:34.270
even I couldn't have foreseen
what it would eventually become.

00:10:34.270 --> 00:10:37.479
But shows the power of a small
group of committed people and

00:10:37.479 --> 00:10:40.701
actually not knowing the odds
of what you're working on.

00:10:40.701 --> 00:10:42.700
So I think both helps.

00:10:42.700 --> 00:10:46.888
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and you can obviously see
the direction that Chrome grew into as

00:10:46.888 --> 00:10:49.306
one of the most popular browsers today.

00:10:49.306 --> 00:10:54.205
And you really have seen Google
transition from pre IPO to

00:10:54.205 --> 00:10:57.791
the trillion dollar company it is today.

00:10:57.791 --> 00:11:01.611
It's challenging to scale
an organization of that size.

00:11:01.611 --> 00:11:07.284
Over the years, what do you think
Google has done well as it scaled,

00:11:07.284 --> 00:11:10.345
&gt;&gt; I mean, the it is a complex thing

00:11:10.345 --> 00:11:16.141
scaling a company,
I would say thinks we have gotten right.

00:11:16.141 --> 00:11:19.523
The first thing that struck me about
Google when I joined the company,

00:11:19.523 --> 00:11:22.921
it was very different in the sense
that it was a very optimistic place.

00:11:22.921 --> 00:11:25.932
So there are places in which
if you walked the hallways and

00:11:25.932 --> 00:11:27.826
you spoke to people in the UN ideas.

00:11:27.826 --> 00:11:29.639
People expanded on them.

00:11:29.639 --> 00:11:33.781
Most of the times people try to tell you
why things won't work I felt the spirit

00:11:33.781 --> 00:11:37.366
was different people will tell
you view That's a great idea.

00:11:37.366 --> 00:11:40.036
You could do it this way and
it would be better.

00:11:40.036 --> 00:11:44.804
So that struck me that optimism,
the fact that you can innovate,

00:11:44.804 --> 00:11:47.491
make things better solve problems,

00:11:47.491 --> 00:11:52.790
I think is the spirit of tried to create
now and it takes a lot of hard work.

00:11:52.790 --> 00:11:56.145
You have to encourage innovation.

00:11:56.145 --> 00:11:58.507
One of the counterintuitive things is,

00:11:58.507 --> 00:12:01.501
companies become more
conservative as they grow.

00:12:01.501 --> 00:12:04.878
They have a lot more cash,
they have a lot more resources.

00:12:04.878 --> 00:12:08.231
But companies tend to become more
conservative in their decision making.

00:12:08.231 --> 00:12:12.825
And so encouraging the company
to take risks and innovate and

00:12:12.825 --> 00:12:17.071
be okay with failure and
reward effort, not outcomes.

00:12:17.071 --> 00:12:21.130
And that's very hard to do in
an organization people tend to reward

00:12:21.130 --> 00:12:21.861
outcomes.

00:12:21.861 --> 00:12:26.183
And which means over time, the
organization becomes more conservative,

00:12:26.183 --> 00:12:28.111
they take safer bets, and so on.

00:12:28.111 --> 00:12:32.274
So a lot of scaling is about making sure
you preserve the good things you had in

00:12:32.274 --> 00:12:35.919
the early days and that gets harder
as the company becomes bigger,

00:12:35.919 --> 00:12:37.561
you're to work harder at it.

00:12:37.561 --> 00:12:42.235
But I think a big part of what we try
hard to do is keep that culture of

00:12:42.235 --> 00:12:45.976
innovating with technology,
building products,

00:12:45.976 --> 00:12:50.661
shipping things, and so
that's one of the many things but yeah.

00:12:50.661 --> 00:12:54.272
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and maintaining that
culture as the company grows.

00:12:54.272 --> 00:12:56.899
Is not easy it's a difficult endeavor.

00:12:56.899 --> 00:13:01.493
What were some of the other kind of
growing pains that you've seen Google have

00:13:01.493 --> 00:13:05.563
over the last couple of years
&gt;&gt; Yeah I mean definitely a lot I mean

00:13:05.563 --> 00:13:07.762
when you're a small company,

00:13:07.762 --> 00:13:13.184
think about the size of the business
schools all of you have shared context.

00:13:13.184 --> 00:13:17.375
You understand better what others
are going through and so you have better

00:13:17.375 --> 00:13:22.321
context around everything that's going on
a larger company definitely gets harder.

00:13:22.321 --> 00:13:25.911
Right.
And Google was built on everyone.

00:13:25.911 --> 00:13:27.581
It's a very open culture.

00:13:27.581 --> 00:13:28.654
Even today.

00:13:28.654 --> 00:13:32.363
One of the most common things people tell
me when they come from other companies is.

00:13:32.363 --> 00:13:35.189
They're shocked at how
transparent the company is,

00:13:35.189 --> 00:13:38.850
you've literally access to what's
happening across the company.

00:13:38.850 --> 00:13:40.606
But it can be overwhelming and

00:13:40.606 --> 00:13:44.689
just because you have transparency
doesn't mean you have context.

00:13:44.689 --> 00:13:48.411
It's very different from when
organizations are smaller.

00:13:48.411 --> 00:13:53.495
So, that's been a big part of trying to
figure out how to scale up the company.

00:13:53.495 --> 00:13:58.499
And how do you organize more independently
coordinate only when needed,

00:13:58.499 --> 00:14:02.462
but can have more parts of
the company move smaller units.

00:14:02.462 --> 00:14:05.101
And that's a hard balance to get right.

00:14:05.101 --> 00:14:07.490
&gt;&gt; And
now you're at the helm of Google and

00:14:07.490 --> 00:14:11.191
you see all of these teams
working in different directions.

00:14:11.191 --> 00:14:16.339
But one thing that you've been vocal
about and enthusiastic about is AI.

00:14:16.339 --> 00:14:21.860
You've said AI is the most important
thing humanity has ever worked on and

00:14:21.860 --> 00:14:27.129
many of us will go on to work with AI or
advanced computing in some form.

00:14:27.129 --> 00:14:33.401
How should we be thinking about AI to
help humanity versus harm humanity?

00:14:33.401 --> 00:14:35.092
&gt;&gt; No, it's a great question.

00:14:35.092 --> 00:14:39.715
When I became CEO, one of the biggest
directional changes we made, as we said,

00:14:39.715 --> 00:14:42.681
we're going to approach
everything as AI first and

00:14:42.681 --> 00:14:46.141
we're applying it across
everything we do in the company.

00:14:46.141 --> 00:14:48.886
It's a big part of the r&amp;d we spend.

00:14:48.886 --> 00:14:52.604
And the progress is palpable every year,

00:14:52.604 --> 00:14:56.543
it's exciting there's a lot of progress.

00:14:56.543 --> 00:15:01.811
I think we concretely see
the evidence of just when

00:15:01.811 --> 00:15:07.086
you look at the scale at
which translation works.

00:15:07.086 --> 00:15:12.191
Or in search how we use AI or in Gmail
when you type and we give suggestions.

00:15:12.191 --> 00:15:14.331
It's applied across all our products.

00:15:14.331 --> 00:15:20.073
And so it definitely, we can see the path
by which we're making things better.

00:15:20.073 --> 00:15:25.029
I think will profoundly transform
pretty much every sector.

00:15:25.029 --> 00:15:29.854
You see the potential in areas
like healthcare I think it'll

00:15:29.854 --> 00:15:34.868
still take a decade for
it to fully play out but we definitely see

00:15:34.868 --> 00:15:40.279
the potential question of how do you
make sure we develop it in a way.

00:15:40.279 --> 00:15:44.717
I think that the essential
struggle of humanity with every

00:15:44.717 --> 00:15:49.170
technology Is harnessing it so
that it benefits society.

00:15:49.170 --> 00:15:52.030
You can see the same
debates about the internet,

00:15:52.030 --> 00:15:56.161
even before you think about AI,
has the internet been a force of good?

00:15:56.161 --> 00:16:01.243
Obviously, has it had effects,
which we didn't fully anticipate?

00:16:01.243 --> 00:16:06.945
Yes, and that's the debate and we
are working about how best to address it.

00:16:06.945 --> 00:16:09.880
With AI I think we need to
think about it earlier.

00:16:09.880 --> 00:16:15.458
So, part of how we are approaching,
we have clearly articulated publicly a set

00:16:15.458 --> 00:16:20.701
of AI principles, and publicly stated
it and we publish a lot of research,

00:16:20.701 --> 00:16:25.386
we open source technology, but
that's only part of the problem.

00:16:25.386 --> 00:16:28.594
I think academic institutions
need to play a big role.

00:16:28.594 --> 00:16:33.982
We were a founding member for
Stanford's AI Institute Hai and

00:16:33.982 --> 00:16:36.900
proud to be a supporter there.

00:16:36.900 --> 00:16:39.328
I think they're doing terrific work.

00:16:39.328 --> 00:16:41.935
But in academic institutions,
nonprofits and

00:16:41.935 --> 00:16:46.300
the public private partnership
government will end up having a roll.

00:16:46.300 --> 00:16:49.424
There has to be thoughtful
regulation about AI.

00:16:49.424 --> 00:16:52.682
You have to get the balance right so
that there is innovation, but

00:16:52.682 --> 00:16:56.610
I think it's important to think it
through earlier than other technologies.

00:16:56.610 --> 00:17:01.993
So I think and so
doing it an engaging all the stakeholders

00:17:01.993 --> 00:17:07.050
is the only way I can
think of approaching it.

00:17:07.050 --> 00:17:11.170
&gt;&gt; So it seems like it's a living
breathing framework that evolves as AI

00:17:11.170 --> 00:17:12.260
also evolves.

00:17:12.260 --> 00:17:15.352
&gt;&gt; That's right.
And it needs to involve many people from

00:17:15.352 --> 00:17:19.400
many different institutions
to make it work.

00:17:19.400 --> 00:17:20.078
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:17:20.078 --> 00:17:24.797
I want to touch on your point on
technology being forward about technology

00:17:24.797 --> 00:17:29.062
and thinking about the benefits and
harms as we're building it.

00:17:29.062 --> 00:17:30.384
In a post COVID world,

00:17:30.384 --> 00:17:34.216
many of us have realized the importance
of human connection, and

00:17:34.216 --> 00:17:38.898
we crave human connection yet sometimes
technology can breed disconnection.

00:17:38.898 --> 00:17:43.475
How are you thinking about the future
of quality connections with each other,

00:17:43.475 --> 00:17:46.400
as technology becomes
a larger part of our lives?

00:17:47.710 --> 00:17:50.901
&gt;&gt; I mean, look technology is an enabler.

00:17:50.901 --> 00:17:57.677
Ultimately it's people in society, we have
to organise around how we use technology.

00:17:57.677 --> 00:18:01.778
I think you're raising
a very important point and,

00:18:01.778 --> 00:18:06.531
thinking through about how
technology is not isolating, or

00:18:06.531 --> 00:18:11.096
immersive in a way in which it
prevents you from engaging,

00:18:11.096 --> 00:18:13.818
I think genuinely is a good topic.

00:18:13.818 --> 00:18:20.265
I think all of us who have kids,worry
about it and struggle about it.

00:18:20.265 --> 00:18:24.862
I did like when our sons were
playing in the middle school band,

00:18:24.862 --> 00:18:27.913
it's what you want to see them do more of.

00:18:27.913 --> 00:18:32.037
But every generation always is very
worried about technology of the future.

00:18:32.037 --> 00:18:35.619
It's always been true
when you look back at it.

00:18:35.619 --> 00:18:39.389
And so I think that's part of the process.

00:18:39.389 --> 00:18:44.159
Technology done correctly,
can enable interactions in the real world.

00:18:44.159 --> 00:18:48.634
One of the companies we spun out of Google
which was Niantic I did that precisely

00:18:48.634 --> 00:18:49.789
that with Pokeyman and

00:18:49.789 --> 00:18:53.812
got people to move about and do stuff in
the real world which I found inspiring.

00:18:53.812 --> 00:18:59.761
But I think over time,
part of even if we get augmented reality,

00:18:59.761 --> 00:19:07.492
right, today when you see people on their
phones walking on the streets immersed.

00:19:07.492 --> 00:19:12.636
You see that in some ways technology
forces you they engage with it.

00:19:12.636 --> 00:19:16.297
It hasn't adapted enough
to how humans live life.

00:19:16.297 --> 00:19:20.935
And so part of solving, more natural
ways by which you can interact with

00:19:20.935 --> 00:19:26.040
computing be it voice be computing,
understanding what you're looking at,

00:19:26.040 --> 00:19:31.141
may actually help us do this better,
than wrongly can be even more isolating,

00:19:31.141 --> 00:19:34.276
but done correctly with
the right attributes.

00:19:34.276 --> 00:19:36.827
I think it can help bridge that gap.

00:19:36.827 --> 00:19:40.538
And so I think that's a potential for
AR if done correctly.

00:19:40.538 --> 00:19:44.234
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so it's thinking in
advance about the implications and

00:19:44.234 --> 00:19:46.964
then being there as
the technology develops.

00:19:46.964 --> 00:19:47.650
&gt;&gt; That's right?

00:19:47.650 --> 00:19:49.163
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:19:49.163 --> 00:19:53.294
I want to take a step back and
look at Google more as a whole.

00:19:53.294 --> 00:19:57.997
Some of the biases we've talked
about with AI but more more broadly,

00:19:57.997 --> 00:20:00.603
what goes on with Google's culture.

00:20:00.603 --> 00:20:06.688
In 2018 employees staged a walkout for
women's rights.

00:20:06.688 --> 00:20:11.008
In 2020 Google was accused of
mishandling the treatment of

00:20:11.008 --> 00:20:13.461
minorities on the ethical AI team.

00:20:13.461 --> 00:20:15.866
To start us off as these
events were happening,

00:20:15.866 --> 00:20:17.618
what was going through your head?

00:20:17.618 --> 00:20:19.696
What was your reaction?

00:20:19.696 --> 00:20:24.631
&gt;&gt; No, one of the fortunate things
have felt as most companies

00:20:24.631 --> 00:20:28.986
from day one Google has had
a strong employee voice.

00:20:28.986 --> 00:20:35.093
And for me as a CEO running a large
company, always found it helpful.

00:20:35.093 --> 00:20:39.756
Because you trust your employees
to get it right at scale.

00:20:39.756 --> 00:20:44.439
So I viewed it as a strength of
the company, when employees speak up.

00:20:44.439 --> 00:20:47.005
I think it's important for
us to take it seriously.

00:20:47.005 --> 00:20:51.044
The walkout was a moment when the company
hadn't gotten something right and

00:20:51.044 --> 00:20:53.010
that's what the walkout was about.

00:20:53.010 --> 00:20:57.830
So internalizing it, acknowledging it,
owning up to it, committing and

00:20:57.830 --> 00:21:01.816
making the company better is
how you approach those moments.

00:21:01.816 --> 00:21:06.865
And even today, I think employee
input is something as a company,

00:21:06.865 --> 00:21:11.642
we value deeply, and I would argue,
they push the company to be

00:21:11.642 --> 00:21:16.799
better across all the things we do
It is complex, as I said earlier.

00:21:16.799 --> 00:21:21.290
The context around some of these
decisions are always hard.

00:21:21.290 --> 00:21:24.566
And at scale,
not everyone has the full context.

00:21:24.566 --> 00:21:25.814
But for what it's worth,

00:21:25.814 --> 00:21:29.840
I've personally always felt one
of the strengths of the company.

00:21:29.840 --> 00:21:34.335
And when it comes to getting AI right or
doing things at scale, and

00:21:34.335 --> 00:21:35.654
getting it right.

00:21:35.654 --> 00:21:37.957
We do it in many countries
around the world.

00:21:37.957 --> 00:21:43.390
And I still today take great comfort
in knowing that our employees deeply

00:21:43.390 --> 00:21:48.569
are guardians of our values and
we'll do everything to get it right.

00:21:48.569 --> 00:21:52.719
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and
I guess these past events help inform

00:21:52.719 --> 00:21:56.159
the future decision making of Google.

00:21:56.159 --> 00:22:00.555
How are you thinking about promoting
DE&amp;I Diversity, Equity and

00:22:00.555 --> 00:22:03.076
Inclusion at Google moving forward?

00:22:03.076 --> 00:22:07.689
&gt;&gt; One of the most important moments
as a company we went through and

00:22:07.689 --> 00:22:12.559
I think many companies around
the world was the racial equity moment

00:22:12.559 --> 00:22:16.589
around the murder of George Floyd,
as a company for us.

00:22:16.589 --> 00:22:18.428
It was a profound moment internally.

00:22:18.428 --> 00:22:24.282
I've never seen anything affect
the company that much in the 16 years.

00:22:24.282 --> 00:22:27.036
We publicly committed to, I consulted and

00:22:27.036 --> 00:22:31.099
work with our black leadership
advisory group in the company.

00:22:31.099 --> 00:22:36.024
And understanding most of the company,
he wanted to do the right thing,

00:22:36.024 --> 00:22:40.560
tapping on the moment and
converting it into lasting commitment.

00:22:40.560 --> 00:22:43.659
So we publicly committed
to a set of initiatives and

00:22:43.659 --> 00:22:46.760
we're holding ourselves accountable.

00:22:46.760 --> 00:22:51.007
We give transparency reports on
how we're making progress on that.

00:22:51.007 --> 00:22:56.494
And be it committing to improving
our leadership representation

00:22:56.494 --> 00:23:03.770
from underrepresented groups committing
to driving improvements in our products.

00:23:03.770 --> 00:23:08.586
Last year in pixel in our camera
technology we launched real tone in

00:23:08.586 --> 00:23:13.321
a more inclusive way to capture
pictures to cover all skin tones.

00:23:13.321 --> 00:23:17.015
I mean, these are all examples
of how you can use technology to

00:23:17.015 --> 00:23:21.420
make progress here scaling and
giving more people access to technology,

00:23:21.420 --> 00:23:24.135
particularly in underrepresented groups.

00:23:24.135 --> 00:23:26.811
These are all commitments we have made.

00:23:26.811 --> 00:23:31.707
And that's one part of how we
work through moments like those.

00:23:31.707 --> 00:23:35.427
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so it seems like you're taking
a technological approach as well as

00:23:35.427 --> 00:23:39.877
an empathetic approach, listening to your
employees, hearing what they have to say.

00:23:39.877 --> 00:23:43.726
What's top of mind for
employees right now is the future of work.

00:23:43.726 --> 00:23:48.460
You recently released your future
of work plan for Google employees.

00:23:48.460 --> 00:23:54.046
And I can imagine you're balancing a lot
of perspectives that are competing.

00:23:54.046 --> 00:23:57.894
How did you integrate competing
preferences when crafting your future of

00:23:57.894 --> 00:23:58.520
plan work?

00:23:59.926 --> 00:24:01.998
For me, for what it's worth,

00:24:01.998 --> 00:24:06.713
I am incredibly excited about this
next face of how the future of work.

00:24:06.713 --> 00:24:11.202
And I think 20 years ago,
Google was kind of, it's now done so

00:24:11.202 --> 00:24:14.961
much around the valley,
people take it for granted.

00:24:14.961 --> 00:24:18.242
But Google did rethink
what workspaces could be.

00:24:18.242 --> 00:24:19.887
They thought workspaces could be fun.

00:24:19.887 --> 00:24:26.826
We had slides in our offices, we bridged
we changed workplaces pretty radically.

00:24:26.826 --> 00:24:30.690
We gave people a lot of agency our
employees had a lot of agency.

00:24:30.690 --> 00:24:33.847
It felt fun to be in the offices.

00:24:33.847 --> 00:24:37.683
And we didn't think that was at
odds of being productive, right.

00:24:37.683 --> 00:24:43.783
So the sense of creating community
fostering creativity in the workplace,

00:24:43.783 --> 00:24:47.606
collaboration all makes
you a better company.

00:24:47.606 --> 00:24:51.734
I view giving flexibility
to people the same way.

00:24:51.734 --> 00:24:56.208
To be very clear I do think we strongly
believe in person connections.

00:24:56.208 --> 00:25:00.849
But I think we can achieve that
in a more purposeful way and

00:25:00.849 --> 00:25:05.114
give employees again more agency and
flexibility.

00:25:05.114 --> 00:25:07.183
So I think hybrid work is great.

00:25:07.183 --> 00:25:09.148
We're going to leverage
the scale of the company.

00:25:09.148 --> 00:25:13.714
We have many locations around the world so
people can move to other places and work.

00:25:13.714 --> 00:25:17.355
We are starting with
a three two hybrid option.

00:25:17.355 --> 00:25:21.187
But we have encouraged employees to
apply to be fully remote as well.

00:25:21.187 --> 00:25:25.397
And we've supported 85%
of those applications.

00:25:25.397 --> 00:25:29.535
So I'm excited,
you earlier asked about diversity.

00:25:29.535 --> 00:25:34.504
One of the best ways we can now approach
diversity is actually showing up in places

00:25:34.504 --> 00:25:36.124
where diverse talent is.

00:25:36.124 --> 00:25:39.417
But more importantly,
when we get that talent then being

00:25:39.417 --> 00:25:43.066
in communities which have
the supporting structure for them.

00:25:43.066 --> 00:25:49.539
And so we are now in Atlanta,
in Chicago, in DC, recruiting employees.

00:25:49.539 --> 00:25:53.743
I think being able to support the
participation of women in the workforce.

00:25:53.743 --> 00:25:56.789
I think the flexibility is
going to be huge asset.

00:25:56.789 --> 00:26:00.621
So I'm excited by it and
I think I find when people come back.

00:26:00.621 --> 00:26:05.252
And people are very excited to come
back to the office, by the way.

00:26:05.252 --> 00:26:10.257
As they opened up,
we are easily at over 70% already back to

00:26:10.257 --> 00:26:15.379
our pre pandemic,
presence in the offices and it's growing.

00:26:15.379 --> 00:26:17.527
But giving people that choice.

00:26:17.527 --> 00:26:21.924
It always bothered me that
the stress around commutes.

00:26:21.924 --> 00:26:25.653
Or when people at parent-teacher
conferences or doctor appointments

00:26:25.653 --> 00:26:29.705
balancing all that, there's a lot of
stress which people who are carrying.

00:26:29.705 --> 00:26:32.936
So, I think it gives us
a chance to rethink.

00:26:32.936 --> 00:26:39.368
Also say technology, since we build
products like Gmail, Docs and so on.

00:26:39.368 --> 00:26:43.440
It gives us a chance to rethink products
too, and make it all work better.

00:26:43.440 --> 00:26:49.349
So, I think it's one of the most
exciting things that's happening in

00:26:49.349 --> 00:26:54.969
the workforce and I think you'll
see the benefits of it over time.

00:26:54.969 --> 00:26:56.200
&gt;&gt; Yeah, absolutely.

00:26:56.200 --> 00:26:59.360
It seems like you're
emphasizing intentionality and

00:26:59.360 --> 00:27:01.838
flexibility when it comes to hybrid work.

00:27:01.838 --> 00:27:04.481
And your plans really do set the stage for

00:27:04.481 --> 00:27:07.603
other companies in Silicon Valley and
beyond.

00:27:07.603 --> 00:27:11.530
So we're all looking to you and
your leadership for

00:27:11.530 --> 00:27:13.912
guidance on things like this.

00:27:13.912 --> 00:27:17.102
So you also recently just
got back from Warsaw,

00:27:17.102 --> 00:27:20.788
you were helping out with
the Ukraine refugee crisis.

00:27:20.788 --> 00:27:24.212
How do you, when is it appropriate for

00:27:24.212 --> 00:27:30.208
corporations to step in on public
issues like Ukraine and Russia?

00:27:30.208 --> 00:27:34.288
&gt;&gt; Well, first of all, for
us we are directly involved

00:27:34.288 --> 00:27:38.570
because both we have employees
in Ukraine and Russia.

00:27:38.570 --> 00:27:43.870
Our products work in these regions,
being an information company at moments

00:27:43.870 --> 00:27:48.770
like this, it's really critical for
us to get these moments, right.

00:27:48.770 --> 00:27:50.483
So we approach it a few ways.

00:27:50.483 --> 00:27:54.673
Foremost, the safety of our employees
like every other organization,

00:27:54.673 --> 00:27:56.605
making sure employees are safe.

00:27:56.605 --> 00:28:00.486
The second very critical for us is
getting access to information, right?

00:28:00.486 --> 00:28:08.029
Tackling misinformation, removing,
what we felt were propaganda information.

00:28:08.029 --> 00:28:09.858
And raising what we do in search and

00:28:09.858 --> 00:28:13.582
YouTube at moments like that is
raising higher quality information,

00:28:13.582 --> 00:28:17.382
including providing information
within Russia at moments like this.

00:28:17.382 --> 00:28:19.984
It's been a large part of the work we do,

00:28:19.984 --> 00:28:24.900
launching important things like air
raid alerts on Google maps in Ukraine.

00:28:24.900 --> 00:28:28.997
Being in Watsa one of the things
that struck me and it's true for

00:28:28.997 --> 00:28:30.930
pretty much everyone there,

00:28:30.930 --> 00:28:35.669
an average Google employee had two
to three refugee families with them.

00:28:35.669 --> 00:28:37.952
These are mainly women and children.

00:28:37.952 --> 00:28:40.663
People don't speak the same
language all the time,

00:28:40.663 --> 00:28:44.188
people are using Google Translate
on their phones to communicate.

00:28:44.188 --> 00:28:47.865
So we have to do a sprint to get
the language translation working right.

00:28:47.865 --> 00:28:52.570
We opened up a portion of our space
to NGOs as well as entrepreneurs from

00:28:52.570 --> 00:28:57.300
Ukraine and more importantly,
we committed to investing in Poland.

00:28:57.300 --> 00:29:00.755
So we announced a $700 million investment,

00:29:00.755 --> 00:29:04.641
both in office space and
to hire more people in Poland.

00:29:04.641 --> 00:29:07.724
And we're going to commit
to investing in Central and

00:29:07.724 --> 00:29:10.028
Eastern Europe at this pivotal time.

00:29:10.028 --> 00:29:15.027
But I think it definitely the war shows
how much we have to continue fighting for

00:29:15.027 --> 00:29:17.997
and so
it's an important moment to get right.

00:29:17.997 --> 00:29:23.458
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it's incredible to see
corporations like Google committing time,

00:29:23.458 --> 00:29:28.847
resources, financial investment,
and technology to causes like this.

00:29:28.847 --> 00:29:33.697
And I'm personally in awe of how
many causes that you're invested in.

00:29:33.697 --> 00:29:35.555
Apart from Ukraine, Russia,

00:29:35.555 --> 00:29:39.490
you also have announced plans
recently in investment in Africa.

00:29:39.490 --> 00:29:43.881
And things like job skills training for
low to middle income folks.

00:29:43.881 --> 00:29:45.020
More broadly,

00:29:45.020 --> 00:29:50.462
how do you choose which causes you
want to invest your time and effort into?

00:29:50.462 --> 00:29:52.134
&gt;&gt; It's a great question.

00:29:52.134 --> 00:29:56.393
With a company at scale, there's a lot of
things that come your way I think you have

00:29:56.393 --> 00:30:00.060
to be disciplined about where you
think Google can make a difference.

00:30:00.060 --> 00:30:06.231
And what is a unique perspective or value
proposition you can bring to the table.

00:30:06.231 --> 00:30:10.530
And over time we have understood
there are things we can do well and

00:30:10.530 --> 00:30:13.984
things other organization's
are better at doing.

00:30:13.984 --> 00:30:17.496
An area, for example,
given our focus on information,

00:30:17.496 --> 00:30:21.022
skilling has been a big focus for
us, digital skilling.

00:30:21.022 --> 00:30:24.838
I think, there's no substitute for
college but

00:30:24.838 --> 00:30:30.803
unfortunately not everyone has access
to it and can afford to go to college.

00:30:30.803 --> 00:30:33.740
And so skilling people bringing them,

00:30:33.740 --> 00:30:38.018
giving them access to digital
skills has been a big push.

00:30:38.018 --> 00:30:41.400
In the US,
we launched a career certificate program.

00:30:41.400 --> 00:30:45.376
It's a nine month program,
we've done it on four major areas.

00:30:45.376 --> 00:30:51.152
And we back it up with working
with employers to recruit people.

00:30:51.152 --> 00:30:56.073
And it's been extraordinarily successful,
75,000 people have gone through it.

00:30:56.073 --> 00:30:59.703
And almost 50% of it is from
underrepresented groups.

00:30:59.703 --> 00:31:04.176
And when I look at the demand there
is from people for these things and

00:31:04.176 --> 00:31:08.571
so I think as a society we need to
figure out about how we can scale and

00:31:08.571 --> 00:31:11.815
give access to digital
skilling to more people.

00:31:11.815 --> 00:31:14.891
But that's an area where we feel
Google can strongly contribute.

00:31:14.891 --> 00:31:16.171
And so we choose and

00:31:16.171 --> 00:31:20.656
get involved in areas like that
sustainability is one of the ones.

00:31:20.656 --> 00:31:24.455
So we choose where we
think we can add value.

00:31:24.455 --> 00:31:28.263
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so it seems like you look at your
strengths, and then look at what impact

00:31:28.263 --> 00:31:31.449
you can make based off of your
strengths and pursue those issues.

00:31:31.449 --> 00:31:32.197
&gt;&gt; That's right.

00:31:32.197 --> 00:31:36.487
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and you touched on this
briefly about sustainability.

00:31:36.487 --> 00:31:37.818
A couple years ago,

00:31:37.818 --> 00:31:42.921
you announced this lofty goal of being
carbon neutral at Google by 2030.

00:31:42.921 --> 00:31:48.801
And you have to unify many business units
and product lines to achieve this goal.

00:31:48.801 --> 00:31:52.797
How are you going about getting the whole
company to rally behind a goal of

00:31:52.797 --> 00:31:53.856
sustainability?

00:31:53.856 --> 00:31:56.591
&gt;&gt; One of the important things and
if I could clarify one thing,

00:31:56.591 --> 00:32:00.910
because it's sadder than people,
we've been carbon neutral since 2007.

00:32:00.910 --> 00:32:04.381
So Google has been carbon
neutral since 2007, and

00:32:04.381 --> 00:32:06.875
one of the earliest companies to do so.

00:32:06.875 --> 00:32:10.060
Sustainability has long
been an important goal.

00:32:10.060 --> 00:32:17.032
What we have now committed is by 2030 to
be carbon free, so not using offsets,

00:32:17.032 --> 00:32:22.750
but actually running our
operations 24/7 carbon free.

00:32:22.750 --> 00:32:27.876
That is a hard challenge,
because today we can use offsets and

00:32:27.876 --> 00:32:33.892
that involves investing in developing
new technologies beyond wind and

00:32:33.892 --> 00:32:41.180
solar, be carbon capture, how do you store
energy and to do it around the world.

00:32:41.180 --> 00:32:45.062
And so, but it excites us because
it's a lot of R&amp;D again, and

00:32:45.062 --> 00:32:47.360
you can apply technology.

00:32:47.360 --> 00:32:52.211
And we bought some of the earliest
wind and solar contracts in 2010,

00:32:52.211 --> 00:32:56.370
and costs have fallen by
85% in the last 10 years.

00:32:56.370 --> 00:32:58.821
And so again,
looking at these new technologies,

00:32:58.821 --> 00:33:02.820
we just have a whole data center
now up and running with geothermal.

00:33:02.820 --> 00:33:06.088
And so, tapping into new technologies, and

00:33:06.088 --> 00:33:10.044
we want to bootstrap it and
help drive the technology and

00:33:10.044 --> 00:33:14.527
the cost curve over the next decade so
that we can reach there.

00:33:14.527 --> 00:33:17.449
But it's a challenge,
it stresses us out, but

00:33:17.449 --> 00:33:21.621
incredibly excited as technologists
the chance to make progress.

00:33:21.621 --> 00:33:27.147
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and none of these big initiatives
were asked of you and asked of Google,

00:33:27.147 --> 00:33:32.775
these are things that you as a company and
you as a leader have decided to take on.

00:33:32.775 --> 00:33:37.990
The theme for our slate of speakers
this year is Beyond Expectations.

00:33:37.990 --> 00:33:41.456
So we'd love to hear what
motivates you to go above and

00:33:41.456 --> 00:33:44.470
beyond what is expected
of a business leader.

00:33:46.760 --> 00:33:48.917
&gt;&gt; I would tie it back
to the first question,

00:33:48.917 --> 00:33:51.210
at least in the context of the work I do.

00:33:51.210 --> 00:33:55.846
I travel around the world, and I still
today, particularly when you go into

00:33:55.846 --> 00:33:59.479
emerging markets,
it's a very different view from here.

00:33:59.479 --> 00:34:04.105
You see how eager people are to
get access to technology because

00:34:04.105 --> 00:34:08.430
they understand how it'll
make their lives better.

00:34:08.430 --> 00:34:13.512
So you see it, you feel it,
when I go to Vietnam, or India,

00:34:13.512 --> 00:34:17.910
or Africa, and
there's a lot more work to be done.

00:34:19.310 --> 00:34:23.678
And just in India recently,
we are working on a cheaper,

00:34:23.678 --> 00:34:28.700
high quality smartphone,
maybe around the $30 price point.

00:34:28.700 --> 00:34:33.845
And last week we just announced in Africa,
our product development center in Nairobi,

00:34:33.845 --> 00:34:37.400
hiring engineers, UX designers and so on.

00:34:37.400 --> 00:34:41.692
When you see the appetite and
the desire for people to make their lives

00:34:41.692 --> 00:34:46.665
better by gaining access to technology,
that's what compels me to go beyond.

00:34:46.665 --> 00:34:50.507
And I think its very consistent with
what our company has set out to do.

00:34:50.507 --> 00:34:54.203
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I can tell how much
intentionality you have behind all

00:34:54.203 --> 00:34:57.610
the initiatives within Google and
externally.

00:34:57.610 --> 00:35:01.000
So truly, thank you so much for
giving us a framework and

00:35:01.000 --> 00:35:05.710
ideas behind how you make decisions,
that we can kind of take away from here.

00:35:06.820 --> 00:35:10.701
With that, we do have student questions,
so we can turn it over.

00:35:13.722 --> 00:35:15.096
Right there.

00:35:15.096 --> 00:35:16.440
&gt;&gt; Hi Sundar.

00:35:16.440 --> 00:35:20.601
My name is Melissa Jiang, and
I was a former Tapestry employee,

00:35:20.601 --> 00:35:21.995
so great to meet you.

00:35:21.995 --> 00:35:27.158
My question is, going back to the year or
forward to the year 2030,

00:35:27.158 --> 00:35:32.678
in what capacity do you see Alphabet
partnering the most with governments?

00:35:32.678 --> 00:35:37.631
And this can be from tech access,
to grids, to search, and

00:35:37.631 --> 00:35:42.003
what will make you feel most
proud of getting right?

00:35:42.003 --> 00:35:47.368
&gt;&gt; The second part is,
I do think sustainability is going to

00:35:47.368 --> 00:35:54.320
be the defining issue for
us to get right in the next decade or so.

00:35:54.320 --> 00:35:59.995
So we are definitely committing Alphabet
seriously, just to get to a carbon

00:35:59.995 --> 00:36:06.470
free goal would involve billions of
dollars in incremental investment from us.

00:36:06.470 --> 00:36:09.505
But we think it's the right thing to do,
it would be good for us as a business,

00:36:09.505 --> 00:36:10.790
I think over time.

00:36:10.790 --> 00:36:15.246
But I think that is something I
want us to be able to get right and

00:36:15.246 --> 00:36:20.241
I think, definitely something we'd
be proud of if we can get there.

00:36:20.241 --> 00:36:22.648
To your first question,
I presume you're asking,

00:36:22.648 --> 00:36:26.530
by 2030, how do you see us engaging
with governments and so on?

00:36:26.530 --> 00:36:31.480
Okay, I think tech, one of the bigger,
more profound changes underway is I think

00:36:31.480 --> 00:36:34.440
technology is going to
be a regulated industry.

00:36:35.980 --> 00:36:39.787
And, part of,
as a company working at scale,

00:36:39.787 --> 00:36:44.976
is anticipating,
working constructively with regulators.

00:36:44.976 --> 00:36:48.575
Because I think at the end of the day,
technology affects citizens and so

00:36:48.575 --> 00:36:51.294
every country is going to be
thinking about this deeply.

00:36:51.294 --> 00:36:53.690
And take Europe's GDPR as an example,

00:36:53.690 --> 00:36:57.693
it's an important foundational
privacy legislation, right?

00:36:57.693 --> 00:36:59.781
And I think we anticipated,

00:36:59.781 --> 00:37:04.218
we invested up almost 18 months
of work to get ready for it.

00:37:04.218 --> 00:37:09.889
And I think it's given certainty to both
European citizens, it gives businesses

00:37:09.889 --> 00:37:15.574
certainty about how to operate, so
that's one example of legislation working.

00:37:15.574 --> 00:37:20.388
And so we would expect to be
constructively working with governments

00:37:20.388 --> 00:37:21.721
around the world.

00:37:21.721 --> 00:37:23.150
Arjuna mentioned AI.

00:37:23.150 --> 00:37:26.820
And I think that's going to be
important for us to get right.

00:37:26.820 --> 00:37:29.627
And I think governments will end up
playing a key role in the timeframe.

00:37:35.701 --> 00:37:39.300
&gt;&gt; Hi, Sundar, I'm Shinies,
thank you for being here.

00:37:39.300 --> 00:37:44.095
Yesterday we had the privilege of
having Barack Obama on campus.

00:37:44.095 --> 00:37:48.458
And he spoke about how changes
in the way we communicate and

00:37:48.458 --> 00:37:53.570
consume information has a massive
impact on our democracy.

00:37:53.570 --> 00:37:57.448
So while Google is not
a social media company,

00:37:57.448 --> 00:38:03.025
I'm curious to know what you see
Google's role in that debate is.

00:38:03.025 --> 00:38:05.254
&gt;&gt; I mean, first of all,
I think it was an important speech.

00:38:05.254 --> 00:38:11.840
And I think that's something we think
about deeply, it's in our stated mission.

00:38:11.840 --> 00:38:16.780
If you think about search, this is what we
are trying to do for every query, right?

00:38:16.780 --> 00:38:21.720
We are trying to sort what is
higher quality information.

00:38:21.720 --> 00:38:25.836
And so,
it cuts to the essence of what we do.

00:38:25.836 --> 00:38:30.643
In YouTube,
we brought the same principles.

00:38:30.643 --> 00:38:33.880
Such a complex topic, but
maybe I can answer it with an example.

00:38:35.690 --> 00:38:41.428
It's actually easier to do this as
a company when society agrees on an area,

00:38:41.428 --> 00:38:44.840
so when society converges and agree.

00:38:44.840 --> 00:38:49.772
You can see that happening in the context
of the war, Russia, Ukraine, and

00:38:49.772 --> 00:38:54.710
I think that's why you see a lot of
companies be able to get it right.

00:38:54.710 --> 00:38:58.537
When society is very divided
about where to draw the lines,

00:38:58.537 --> 00:39:01.150
it inherently gets harder.

00:39:01.150 --> 00:39:06.070
But the way we approach is generally
on important areas, to use YouTube

00:39:06.070 --> 00:39:11.500
as an example, we raise what we think
of as authoritative information.

00:39:11.500 --> 00:39:17.305
That's journalistic content, or
in the case of, if it's health related,

00:39:17.305 --> 00:39:22.773
maybe from universities or hospitals or
public health organizations.

00:39:22.773 --> 00:39:27.539
So we use those tools just like
we have done search to try and

00:39:27.539 --> 00:39:33.410
think hard about what are higher
quality sources of information.

00:39:33.410 --> 00:39:38.410
And we view that as a goal, and that's
one way by which we tackle the problem.

00:39:38.410 --> 00:39:43.179
But it's an important issue and I think
part of it is, if there are better rules,

00:39:43.179 --> 00:39:47.620
including laws and legislation,
I think it'll actually make it easier.

00:39:47.620 --> 00:39:52.148
But you'll find writing the law and
legislation is hard, because I think as

00:39:52.148 --> 00:39:56.677
a society, we're still grappling with
what we think is the right answer for

00:39:56.677 --> 00:39:57.970
many of these things.

00:40:11.400 --> 00:40:12.232
Hi, Sundar.

00:40:12.232 --> 00:40:14.680
I'm the at the business school.

00:40:14.680 --> 00:40:16.898
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And also from Madras, so

00:40:16.898 --> 00:40:18.210
it's really nice to meet you.

00:40:18.210 --> 00:40:22.117
I used to work for the Gates Foundation
thinking about bringing innovation to

00:40:22.117 --> 00:40:24.233
emerging markets in undeserved people.

00:40:24.233 --> 00:40:25.585
So everything you say resonates.

00:40:25.585 --> 00:40:27.454
My question is a more personal one.

00:40:27.454 --> 00:40:31.503
During the work you do, there are many
difficulties, difficult moments,

00:40:31.503 --> 00:40:32.398
highs and lows.

00:40:32.398 --> 00:40:34.342
Do you have any everyday habits or

00:40:34.342 --> 00:40:37.889
personal mantras that keep you
going when things get tough?

00:40:40.080 --> 00:40:41.555
&gt;&gt; It's a great question.

00:40:41.555 --> 00:40:46.095
First of all, in my most decisions
it took me a while to realize when

00:40:46.095 --> 00:40:50.556
decisions come to you,
the higher up you are in an organization,

00:40:50.556 --> 00:40:53.821
the easy decisions don't come to you,
right?

00:40:53.821 --> 00:40:57.450
[LAUGH] By definition when
something has come to you,

00:40:57.450 --> 00:41:02.325
it's because others have spent time
on it and they can't resolve it.

00:41:02.325 --> 00:41:04.469
So in some ways I realized that.

00:41:04.469 --> 00:41:06.081
So two things, one is,

00:41:06.081 --> 00:41:10.926
I think you making that decision is
the most important thing you can do.

00:41:10.926 --> 00:41:17.511
You're breaking a tie and it unlocks
the organization to move forward.

00:41:17.511 --> 00:41:21.699
And so, it's an important thing.

00:41:21.699 --> 00:41:25.210
One of the mentors here, Bill Campbell,
taught that to me early.

00:41:25.210 --> 00:41:29.335
Every week he would see me, he would ask
me, what ties did you break this week?

00:41:29.335 --> 00:41:31.338
And so it's always struck with me.

00:41:31.338 --> 00:41:34.408
So I view making these decisions as,

00:41:34.408 --> 00:41:40.457
you're really helping the company,
and so that makes it a bit more fun.

00:41:40.457 --> 00:41:46.638
The second is, with time you realize most
of those decisions are inconsequential.

00:41:46.638 --> 00:41:49.584
It might appear very tough at the time.

00:41:49.584 --> 00:41:53.698
It may feel like a lot rides on it,
prelook later and

00:41:53.698 --> 00:41:57.358
you realize it wasn't that consequential.

00:41:57.358 --> 00:42:02.503
There are few consequential decisions,
and judgment is a big part of leadership,

00:42:02.503 --> 00:42:06.411
and you don't always get it right and
you have to learn from it.

00:42:06.411 --> 00:42:09.633
But I think most decisions
aren't that consequential.

00:42:09.633 --> 00:42:14.564
And so thinking through both,
it helps me think about it as it's just

00:42:14.564 --> 00:42:19.430
another normal day in the office,
and so you keep going through it.

00:42:19.430 --> 00:42:22.866
&gt;&gt; So I think we have time for
one more question right back there.

00:42:25.832 --> 00:42:26.526
&gt;&gt; Hi, Sundar.

00:42:26.526 --> 00:42:28.380
My name is Nick Pershore.

00:42:28.380 --> 00:42:29.696
I'm from Syria.

00:42:29.696 --> 00:42:33.685
And in Syria,
20 million people don't have access

00:42:33.685 --> 00:42:37.066
to all of the products
that Google produces.

00:42:37.066 --> 00:42:40.727
That's because of regulations that
are put in place by countries like

00:42:40.727 --> 00:42:44.091
the United States and
certain countries of the European Union.

00:42:44.091 --> 00:42:47.871
So on top of being under a dictatorship,
20 million Syrians don't have access to

00:42:47.871 --> 00:42:51.230
the financial world, don't have
access to basic technology products.

00:42:51.230 --> 00:42:53.577
And the gap between the developed and

00:42:53.577 --> 00:42:56.540
developing world is
really just increasing.

00:42:56.540 --> 00:42:59.405
You're one of the most
important leaders in the world.

00:42:59.405 --> 00:43:02.871
What are you doing to advocate
on behalf of these people and

00:43:02.871 --> 00:43:06.144
give truly universal access
to technology products?

00:43:06.144 --> 00:43:07.531
Thank you.

00:43:07.531 --> 00:43:09.059
&gt;&gt; That's a great question.

00:43:09.059 --> 00:43:14.624
As a company, our mission is to provide
universal access to information,

00:43:14.624 --> 00:43:18.879
and anytime we are not able to do that for
a set of reasons.

00:43:18.879 --> 00:43:24.190
I mean, we struggle with it,
we feel compelled to try to find a way.

00:43:24.190 --> 00:43:26.783
We do have to comply
with laws as a company.

00:43:26.783 --> 00:43:32.256
And in areas like Syria,
the ways we have contributed,

00:43:32.256 --> 00:43:37.497
a, we've been involved in
the refugee workaround,

00:43:37.497 --> 00:43:40.764
Syrian refugees for a long time.

00:43:40.764 --> 00:43:45.877
We build access to open source
technologies in many of these cases and

00:43:45.877 --> 00:43:47.207
support things.

00:43:47.207 --> 00:43:51.316
Android is open source, things do
make their way into these countries.

00:43:51.316 --> 00:43:55.900
So we work at the open source level in
areas where we are not fully able to

00:43:55.900 --> 00:43:57.000
directly work.

00:43:57.000 --> 00:43:59.080
But I think it's an important question.

00:43:59.080 --> 00:44:00.713
I don't have all the answers here.

00:44:00.713 --> 00:44:06.116
But thanks for asking it, and
I'll think about it more.

00:44:06.116 --> 00:44:11.150
&gt;&gt; Awesome, so we typically conclude our
conversations with a lightning round.

00:44:11.150 --> 00:44:13.975
I will start a sentence and
you will finish it.

00:44:13.975 --> 00:44:15.327
So it's a fill in the blank.

00:44:15.327 --> 00:44:19.610
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So first of all,

00:44:19.610 --> 00:44:24.011
something that inspires me is?

00:44:24.011 --> 00:44:26.357
&gt;&gt; Watching the next generation blossom.

00:44:26.357 --> 00:44:30.893
And I think society always
worries that the next generation

00:44:30.893 --> 00:44:34.351
isn't as good as they are,
that is not true.

00:44:34.351 --> 00:44:36.823
And the next generation ends up
making the world even better.

00:44:36.823 --> 00:44:38.982
So that inspires me.

00:44:38.982 --> 00:44:41.427
&gt;&gt; I am the most proud of?

00:44:43.650 --> 00:44:48.334
&gt;&gt; Trying my best to do the right
thing by the people I'm involved with,

00:44:48.334 --> 00:44:53.288
both obviously, personally and
professionally, people I work with.

00:44:53.288 --> 00:44:57.001
&gt;&gt; During my time at Stanford, I loved?

00:44:57.001 --> 00:44:59.874
&gt;&gt; I used to love just
being out in the court, and

00:44:59.874 --> 00:45:02.608
sitting and grabbing lunch with friends.

00:45:02.608 --> 00:45:05.910
Driving today reminded me of that,
for sure.

00:45:05.910 --> 00:45:08.774
&gt;&gt; I am the happiest when?

00:45:08.774 --> 00:45:14.338
&gt;&gt; Many things, but
being around people, building products,

00:45:14.338 --> 00:45:18.193
and solving problems makes me very happy.

00:45:18.193 --> 00:45:24.867
&gt;&gt; And finally, the best piece of
leadership advice I've received is?

00:45:24.867 --> 00:45:29.513
&gt;&gt; Be authentic to yourself and
be the best leader you can be.

00:45:29.513 --> 00:45:35.894
I think there's no one right template, and
don't try to be in someone else's mold.

00:45:35.894 --> 00:45:36.960
&gt;&gt; That's wonderful.

00:45:36.960 --> 00:45:38.366
Sundar, thank you so much.

00:45:38.366 --> 00:45:39.438
It's been such a pleasure.

00:45:39.438 --> 00:45:45.523
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:45:45.523 --> 00:45:50.694
[MUSIC]

